# PROJECT_NAME — agent context

> Generated by `orch init --template expo-mobile` (GENERATED_AT).

## About orch (this project is orch-managed)

- `orch` walks `tasks.json` as a DAG and dispatches each task to
  Claude / Codex / OpenCode / Gemini in parallel.
- **CLI missing?** `pipx install orch`
  (repo: <https://github.com/hectorcanaimero/orch>).
- **Never edit `tasks.json` status directly** — runtime state lives in
  SQLite. Use `orch task set --id ID --status STATUS` or one of the
  `scripts/task-*.sh` helpers.
- **Full agent context on demand**: if you use Claude Code, run
  `orch install-skills` once — the `/orch` skill loads only when the
  agent needs it (token-cheap). Otherwise `orch --help` is the source
  of truth.

## Stack

- Expo SDK, pinned — not a caret range. A minor bump can change the
  native runtime under you, and nothing in CI would catch it.
- `expo-router` for file-based navigation; `main` in package.json is
  `expo-router/entry`, not the default `index.js`.
- TypeScript in strict mode.
- `jest` with the **`jest-expo` preset**. The default `jsdom`
  environment cannot render React Native components — a test suite
  configured without this preset fails on the first `render()` with an
  error that points nowhere near the cause.
- `@react-native-async-storage/async-storage` for persistence, with the
  library's own jest mock.

## Layout

- `app/` — routes. The directory IS the navigation tree: `app/_layout.tsx`
  is the root stack, `app/(tabs)/` is a tab group, and a file's path is
  its URL.
- `components/` — shared presentational components
- `hooks/` — stateful logic that more than one screen needs
- `lib/` — non-React code (API client, storage) with no imports from `app/`
- `constants/` — colours, spacing, anything with no behaviour
- `__tests__/` — jest specs
- `.orchestrator/` — orch state (config, model router, SQLite)

## Conventions

- Every screen change ships with a test in the same PR — no exceptions.
- `npm run typecheck && npm test` before pushing; CI runs the same two.
- Nothing in `lib/` imports from `app/`. The dependency points one way,
  so the data layer stays testable without a renderer.
- Colours come from `constants/Colors.ts` through the themed components.
  A hard-coded hex in a screen is a bug in both colour schemes at once.

## What CI does not do

There is no simulator and no device on the runner, so `expo start`,
`expo run:ios` and `expo run:android` are your machine's job. CI
type-checks and runs unit tests. A task whose acceptance criteria need a
running app has to say how to verify it by hand — write that in the spec.

## Orch state

- SQLite at `.orchestrator/state/PROJECT_NAME/orch.db`.
- `tasks_definition` (static): id, model, backend, deps, phase, estimate.
- `tasks_runtime` (dynamic): status, attempts, last_model, last_backend.
- **Never edit runtime status in `tasks.json`** — use `orch task set --id ID --status STATUS` instead.

## Useful commands

```bash
orch status                              # human table
orch tasks --status todo,in-progress     # narrower view
orch run --dry-run                       # preview the plan
orch dashboard --profile stakeholder     # share the URL with your client
```
